        {
          "question": "What is the sieve of Eratosthenes?",
          "answer": "The sieve of Eratosthenes is an ancient algorithm for finding all prime numbers up to a specified limit by iteratively marking multiples of each prime, starting with 2, then moving to the next unmarked number which is declared prime, continuing until all numbers are processed"
        },
        {
          "question": "What is modular arithmetic?",
          "answer": "Modular arithmetic is a system of arithmetic for integers where numbers 'wrap around' when reaching a certain value called the modulus. For example, in modulo 12 (clock arithmetic), 14:00 is equivalent to 2:00"
        },
        {
          "question": "What are Carmichael numbers?",
          "answer": "Carmichael numbers are composite (non-prime) numbers that satisfy Fermat's Little Theorem for all bases that are coprime to the number. They are also known as Fermat pseudoprimes and are important in primality testing"
        },
        {
          "question": "What is the Prime Number Theorem?",
          "answer": "The Prime Number Theorem describes the asymptotic distribution of prime numbers, stating that the number of primes less than or equal to a large number n is approximately n/ln(n), where ln(n) is the natural logarithm of n"
        },
        {
          "question": "What is Euler's totient function?",
          "answer": "Euler's totient function φ(n) counts the positive integers up to n that are coprime to n (i.e., their greatest common divisor with n is 1). It plays a crucial role in number theory and cryptography"
        },
        {
          "question": "What are congruence relations?",
          "answer": "Congruence relations are equivalence relations in modular arithmetic. Two integers a and b are congruent modulo n (written a ≡ b (mod n)) if their difference a-b is divisible by n, or equivalently, if they have the same remainder when divided by n"
        },
        {
          "question": "What is the Chinese Remainder Theorem?",
          "answer": "The Chinese Remainder Theorem states that if one has a system of congruences x ≡ a₁ (mod m₁), x ≡ a₂ (mod m₂), ..., x ≡ aₙ (mod mₙ) where all moduli m₁, m₂, ..., mₙ are pairwise coprime, then there exists a unique solution x modulo the product M = m₁×m₂×...×mₙ"
        },
        {
          "question": "What is the connection between Mersenne primes and perfect numbers?",
          "answer": "When a Mersenne number 2ᵖ-1 is prime (making it a Mersenne prime), the number 2ᵖ⁻¹(2ᵖ-1) is a perfect number. Euclid proved that all even perfect numbers have this form, and it's conjectured that no odd perfect numbers exist"
        },
        {
          "question": "What is the Goldbach Conjecture?",
          "answer": "The Goldbach Conjecture states that every even integer greater than 2 can be expressed as the sum of two prime numbers. It remains one of the oldest and most famous unsolved problems in number theory"
        },
        {
          "question": "What is the Riemann Hypothesis?",
          "answer": "The Riemann Hypothesis is a conjecture that all non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function have a real part equal to 1/2. It has profound implications for the distribution of prime numbers and is considered one of the most important unsolved problems in mathematics"
        },
        {
          "question": "What is the RSA algorithm?",
          "answer": "RSA is a public-key cryptographic algorithm based on the difficulty of factoring large composite numbers. It uses a public key for encryption and a private key for decryption, with both keys generated using large prime numbers"
        },
        {
          "question": "What is Fermat's Little Theorem?",
          "answer": "Fermat's Little Theorem states that if p is a prime number and a is any integer not divisible by p, then aᵖ⁻¹ ≡ 1 (mod p). Equivalently, aᵖ ≡ a (mod p) for any integer a"
        },
        {
          "question": "What is a Diophantine equation?",
