          "answer": "Cell movement involves coordinated cytoskeletal dynamics where actin polymerization at the leading edge pushes the membrane forward, focal adhesions form to anchor the cell to the substrate, myosin motors contract the actin network to pull the cell body forward, and focal adhesions at the rear disassemble to release trailing attachments. This process requires precise spatial and temporal regulation through signaling pathways that control cytoskeletal assembly, contractility, and adhesion dynamics in response to directional cues"
        },
        
        {
          "question": "How do mitochondria and chloroplasts reflect their evolutionary origins?",
          "answer": "Mitochondria and chloroplasts reflect their bacterial origins through multiple features: they contain circular DNA genomes similar to bacteria; they divide through binary fission independent of cell division; they possess their own translation machinery with ribosomes resembling bacterial ribosomes; they maintain double-membrane structures with the inner membrane resembling bacterial plasma membranes; and they show sequence homology between their genes and those of their bacterial ancestors—all supporting the endosymbiotic theory of their evolution from free-living prokaryotes"
        },
        
        {
          "question": "What are the molecular mechanisms of cellular senescence?",
          "answer": "Cellular senescence involves complex molecular mechanisms including telomere shortening that triggers DNA damage responses, activation of tumor suppressor pathways (p53/p21 and p16/Rb) that enforce cell cycle arrest, widespread chromatin remodeling leading to senescence-associated heterochromatin foci, metabolic reprogramming with mitochondrial dysfunction, and development of the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) that alters the tissue microenvironment—collectively representing a stress response that prevents malignant transformation but contributes to aging"
        },
        
        {
