    {
      "context": "Geometry is the branch of mathematics concerned with the properties and relations of points, lines, surfaces, solids, and higher dimensional analogs. It provides a framework for understanding spatial relationships and forms the foundation for many practical applications in science, engineering, art, and architecture.\n\nEuclidean geometry, developed in ancient Greece, deals with flat (planar) spaces and three-dimensional space based on Euclid's axioms and postulates. Key concepts include points, lines, angles, triangles, circles, and polygons. Fundamental principles like the Pythagorean theorem, angle relationships, congruence, and similarity enable the analysis of shapes and their properties. Measurement formulas for area, volume, and perimeter are essential tools for quantifying geometric objects.\n\nBeyond Euclidean geometry, non-Euclidean geometries explore spaces with different properties. Spherical geometry examines figures on the surface of a sphere, where straight lines are great circles and the sum of a triangle's angles exceeds 180 degrees. Hyperbolic geometry studies spaces with negative curvature, where parallel lines diverge and triangles have angle sums less than 180 degrees.\n\nAnalytic geometry, pioneered by Descartes, merges algebra and geometry by using coordinate systems to represent geometric objects algebraically. This branch enables the mathematical analysis of curves, surfaces, and transformations. Differential geometry examines curved spaces using calculus and is fundamental to Einstein's theory of relativity. Topology, often called \"rubber-sheet geometry,\" focuses on properties that remain unchanged under continuous deformations like stretching and bending.\n\nComputational geometry applies algorithms to solve geometric problems, with applications in computer graphics, geographic information systems, and robotics. Projective geometry studies properties preserved under projection, essential in perspective drawing and computer vision. Modern applications of geometry span diverse fields from medical imaging and architectural design to animation and virtual reality.",
